remember when you could order something  online and it would be there the next  day remember when there were more people  looking for jobs than there were job  openings those things were all the  result of efficient markets supply and  demand being in Balance but today  markets are out of whack in just about  every Market you're seeing a ton of  demand and Supply that just isn't able  to meet it I wanted to understand why  this is still happening more than two  years after the pandemic caused Mass  shutdowns so I spoke to some economists  and researchers it turns out that kova  didn't change the economy it pushed  forward and exacerbated a lot of issues  that were already there what could this  mean I'll explain  as we know markets function because when  demand Rises prices rise and that  motivates producers to increase Supply  but the supply hasn't shown up the auto  market is a great example  because new car production has slowed so  much Americans looking to replace their  vehicle are shelling out more for some  used car models than brand new ones but  at least with cars you've got a used  Market to buy from with some other  products like diapers baby formula and  toilet paper customers have just had to  go without them or wait I spoke with  Aaron Parrott a managing director at  consulting firm Deloitte he told me that  businesses aren't investing in supply  for a lot of products right now because  they don't believe demand will be  long-term and sustainable if it takes  more than a year to build out the  capacity parrot tells me that companies  would rather miss out on the potential  windfall because they don't want to take  the risk retailers like Target and  Walmart learned this the hard way  recently disclosing to investors that  they had to mark down billions of  dollars in merchandise because taste had  changed and Garvin Jay Bush Chief  investment officer for green Alpha  Advisor says there's another problem he  says companies were simply caught  flat-footed for years before the  pandemic they were too focused on  short-term profit goals and and didn't  invest sufficiently in emerging Trends  or research and development he points to  data that show in the Years leading up  to 2020 U.S companies spent more money  buying back their own stock and paying  dividends to investors than they did on  R D similar issues plagued the U.S labor  market we're seeing almost two job  postings for every unemployed person in  the country that ratio had never even  hit one to one in the years before 2018.  much of this has to do with what ADP  Chief Economist Nila Richardson calls a  mismatched economy where there are lots  of available workers but few with the  skills companies are looking for  Richardson says that because interest  rates were so low for so long and  companies could see their stock prices  rise without having to invest much in  hiring workers or training them many got  complacent now companies are looking to  hire lots more workers to help them  transition to the economy of the future  and finding that getting employees with  the skills they want is a tough task the  pandemic also caused many Americans to  rethink their work Arrangements because  of healthcare stories and the rising  cost of care the combination of health  and economic concerns the skills  mismatch and early retirements have  driven the percentage of Americans in  the labor force to the lowest since the  1970s even with nominal wages rising at  the fastest Pace in the years the big  picture is that covid made companies  deal with issues that many thought were  far off in the future today and even  though stock prices have fallen this  year most large firms are still flush  with cash and seem reluctant to rock the  boat the government hasn't been able to  step in to fill the void so the void is  just there the current environment is  leaving a lot of consumers without  Solutions and a lot of businesses  without workers but it does seem like  the tide i